  url = {https://www.cell.com/cell/abstract/S0092-8674(21)00171-9},
  urldate = {2023-10-06},
  abstract = {{$<$}h2{$>$}Summary{$<$}/h2{$><$}p{$>$}How early events in effector T cell (T\textsubscript{EFF}) subsets tune memory T cell (T\textsubscript{MEM}) responses remains incompletely understood. Here, we systematically investigated metabolic factors in fate determination of T\textsubscript{EFF} and T\textsubscript{MEM} cells using \mkbibemph{in vivo} pooled CRISPR screening, focusing on negative regulators of T\textsubscript{MEM} responses. We found that amino acid transporters Slc7a1 and Slc38a2 dampened the magnitude of T\textsubscript{MEM} differentiation, in part through modulating mTORC1 signaling. By integrating genetic and systems approaches, we identified cellular and metabolic heterogeneity among T\textsubscript{EFF} cells, with terminal effector differentiation associated with establishment of metabolic quiescence and exit from the cell cycle. Importantly, Pofut1 (protein-O-fucosyltransferase-1) linked GDP-fucose availability to downstream Notch-Rbpj signaling, and perturbation of this nutrient signaling axis blocked terminal effector differentiation but drove context-dependent T\textsubscript{EFF} proliferation and T\textsubscript{MEM} development. Our study establishes that nutrient uptake and signaling are key determinants of T cell fate and shape the quantity and quality of T\textsubscript{MEM} responses.{$<$}/p{$>$}},
